            <p>
              Wednesday, 28 October, marked 700 days since the current wave of
              protests began in Georgia, triggered by the decision to suspend
              the process of opening EU accession negotiations. It followed
              demonstrations that had already begun after the parliamentary
              elections of October 2024, widely regarded as fraudulent.
            </p>

            <p>
              On 26 October 2024, Georgia held parliamentary elections in which
              Georgian Dream (GD) was declared the winner with approximately
              54 per cent of the vote. The result was widely contested, and
              even the country's president, Salome Zourabichvili, called for an
              investigation and new elections. And for good reason.
              International election observers found widespread pressure and
              intimidation directed at voters in the run-up to the election,
              concerns about ballot secrecy and inconsistencies in the
              electoral process. The European Parliament also described the
              election in a resolution as marred by serious irregularities and
              stated that it had been neither free nor fair. Public trust in
              society and its institutions has been seriously undermined.
            </p>

            <p>
              Georgian Dream's policies have been criticised as serving
              Russian interests and seeking to concentrate power. Once in
              power, the party has introduced a raft of measures that, in its
              own interpretation, promote national security and domestic
              harmony. So how do they think that went? In the great public
              effort to uphold law and justice, everyone seems to have had to
              sacrifice something. Civil society organisations, the opposition
              and the media have sacrificed their freedoms, funding and ability
              to operate. Georgian Dream, in its boundless generosity,
              sacrificed its citizens' rights and freedoms instead. Something
              for everyone.

              The party has justified its actions as defending national
              security, sovereignty and social stability. In practice, it has
              enacted laws requiring certain organisations and media outlets
              receiving significant foreign funding to register as
              representing the interests of a foreign power. Subsequent
              legislative amendments have also made the receipt of foreign
              grants conditional on government approval. Rules governing
              demonstrations have been tightened, and the operating space for
              independent media has been narrowed.

              When civil society funding is brought under government control,
              critical media are restricted and the risks of taking to the
              streets increase, we are no longer talking about isolated
              administrative reforms. We are talking about the kind of
              relationship between the state and its citizens that the
              government wants to build.

              The party is closely associated with oligarchic power. GD does
              not, of course, describe itself as pro-Russian, but its actions
              suggest that its policies serve Russia's objectives. The
              government announced that it would suspend the process aimed at
              opening EU accession negotiations until 2028. Its actions have
              also weakened relations with the West and served Russia's
              interests in determining Georgia's future direction. Its methods
              bear many similarities to the Russian approach to the opposition
              and civic activism. The independence of the judiciary has also
              been called into question. The influence of political power over
              the courts, the prosecution service and corruption investigations
              has repeatedly come under criticism.
            </p>

            <p>
              That brings us to the protests themselves. The reasons behind
              them are understandable. Georgian citizens want a country where
              democracy and the rule of law are respected. They want Georgia to
              be an EU member, even though there is still a long way to go.
              The protesters are not simply opposing individual laws or
              government decisions. They are demanding that Georgia return to a
              democratic path, that the rule of law and civil liberties be
              protected, and that they be able to influence their country's
              future without fear of intimidation or violence.

              For many, Georgia's European direction is also central to the
              issue. EU membership is not merely a foreign policy objective:
              it represents the hope of a fairer society, a functioning
              democracy and protection against arbitrary government power.
              Their demands have also included new, genuinely free and fair
              elections and accountability for the violence directed at
              protesters. This is not just about who governs the country, but
              also about the terms on which it is governed.
            </p>

            <p>
              Given the circumstances, citizens have been protesting
              relatively peacefully. The demonstrations have largely remained
              peaceful despite the authorities' heavy-handed response. People
              are simply desperate. The persistence of the protests has been
              summed up in a question: what else can we do when the rule of law
              and a safe society are being taken away from us?

              The authorities have treated protesters with extreme violence
              and unfairness. People have been subjected to disproportionate
              force, arbitrary arrests and beatings, including after being
              detained. Women protesters have reported sexual threats,
              harassment and violence. In December 2024, Georgian civil society
              organisations issued a joint statement describing reports by
              women demonstrators of rape threats, sexual harassment and other
              degrading treatment during detention.

              In Georgia, opposition politicians' homes have been searched,
              and politicians have been imprisoned on grounds widely regarded
              as politically motivated. Similar measures have also been
              directed at other civic activists. Yet people continue to
              protest, even though the personal consequences for them can be
              severe. This shows how much they feel their own freedom and
              their country's future are at stake. And I cannot blame them,
              given how little the current government has done to earn their
              trust.
            </p>

            <p>
              So why is a political novice from Finland writing about this?
              I know something about the history and ways of thinking in
              countries that emerged from the Soviet Union. Russia invaded
              Georgia in 2008. As a result, Russia continues to occupy parts
              of Georgia's internationally recognised territory in Abkhazia
              and South Ossetia.

              In light of my country's history, I feel sympathy for every
              country that has to navigate life next to Russia without the
              security that NATO membership provides. I have Georgians in my
              wider circle who have asked me to help raise awareness of the
              situation in their homeland. I am happy to do so.

              This is not an in-depth analysis. I want to give space to the
              stories and demands of the people demonstrating in Tbilisi for
              their lives and rights, come rain or shine. I hope that one
              beautiful day, Georgia, on the eastern shore of the Black Sea,
              will be an EU member state where the rule of law and human
              rights are upheld.

              I believe that a democratic transfer of power and a stronger
              rule of law are possible in Georgia in the future. The current
              direction of travel, however, offers no guarantees. What keeps
              hope alive is that civil society and protesters continue their
              work despite the growing risks.
            </p>

